WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.850
We just are such lucky people that we get to eat this food

2
00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:06.240
and celebrate these restaurants and what a better way

3
00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:10.967
to kick off our week celebrating meat than with.

4
00:00:10.967 --> 00:00:15.220
(upbeat music)
(audience applauding)

5
00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:16.070
Barbecue ribs.

6
00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:17.827
Oh, please, thank you so much.

7
00:00:17.827 --> 00:00:22.250
There are so many amazing barbecue places around the US,

8
00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:24.110
and we couldn't possibly feature them all,

9
00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:27.440
but we're going to try to cover the three bases,

10
00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.180
with three different restaurants in three different cities.

11
00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:34.820
But first, we need our taste-testing partner in crime,

12
00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:35.800
Ross Matthews.

13
00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:39.501
Well, hi there. Hi, fellow barbecutie.

14
00:00:39.501 --> 00:00:40.660
(Ross giggling)

15
00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.740
Um, all right, let's not forget the stakes.

16
00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:46.630
Yeah, that's a meat pun, the stakes being the spot

17
00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:51.205
with the most votes wins our Battle of the Bites trophy!

18
00:00:51.205 --> 00:00:53.240
Yeah, oh, wow.
(grand music)

19
00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:54.780
Isn't it wonderful?

20
00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.730
Ross, are you a fan of barbecue?

21
00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:58.821
I don't have a beef with it.

22
00:00:58.821 --> 00:01:00.290
(Ross chuckling)

23
00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:01.670
No, of course, I love barbecue.

24
00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:03.230
Whenever I'm in any of these states,

25
00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:05.000
I have to get the barbecue.

26
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.470
It's the best of the best, and they do it so differently,

27
00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:08.980
so this is going to be quite the battle.

28
00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:12.150
And now joining us for this taste test are three

29
00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:17.150
of our VFFs: hello to Adrian, Emily, and Mable.

30
00:01:17.185 --> 00:01:19.425
(audience applauding)

31
00:01:19.425 --> 00:01:22.069
Hi, guys! Hi, guys!

32
00:01:22.069 --> 00:01:22.902
Hi!

33
00:01:22.902 --> 00:01:24.500
We're so excited to do this with you.

34
00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:27.040
And of course our final vote in the competition

35
00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:30.010
comes from our social media fans.

36
00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:32.840
We put up a Twitter poll asking everyone at home to vote

37
00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:36.690
for their favorite, and we'll count that as one vote

38
00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:39.780
towards who becomes the winner at the end of this.

39
00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:40.900
All right, let's get started.

40
00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.140
Now, we each have three plates of ribs

41
00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:44.790
with a signature side.

42
00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:46.190
All right, I'm so excited.

43
00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:48.940
Our first up is Kings Restaurant.

44
00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:50.133
Please take a look.

45
00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:54.120 line:15%
Hello, my name is Joe Hargitt and I am the owner

46
00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.830
of Kings Restaurant, here in Kinston, North Carolina.

47
00:01:56.830 --> 00:01:58.697
The business started off in 1936

48
00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:01.930 line:15%
by a fellow named Frank King,

49
00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:04.350 line:15%
and he had a son named Wilbur Senior.

50
00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:06.770
He brought his two brothers into the business

51
00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:10.340
and hence we became Kings Brothers Barbecue.

52
00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:13.040
Today, we're going to talk about our baby back pork ribs,

53
00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.900
how tender they are, and we smoke 'em here in-house,

54
00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:17.230
a Applewood smoke.

55
00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:19.210
They're so tender, they fall off the bone

56
00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:21.100
before they even get into the smoker.

57
00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:24.470
We don't use any fancy rubs or anything like that.

58
00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:26.730
We just use a really good, homemade

59
00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:28.590
Eastern North Carolina barbecue sauce.

60
00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:33.590
Last year we sold 39,000 pounds of baby back pork ribs.

61
00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:37.010
Our featured side that we've decided to use

62
00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:38.460
is collard greens.

63
00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:41.520
It is a Southern staple, a family recipe that has been

64
00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.520
in Kings Restaurant for almost 85 years.

65
00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.870
We get our collard greens from a local farmer,

66
00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:48.410
not 20 miles away.

67
00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:52.290
We cook over 3,000 pounds of collard greens a week.

68
00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:55.780
We supply restaurants all over Eastern North Carolina

69
00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:57.390
with our collard greens.

70
00:02:57.390 --> 00:03:00.640
So in this difficult time, when travel was restricted,

71
00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:03.650
let pigs fly right into your house

72
00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:05.443
and land on your taste buds.

73
00:03:05.443 --> 00:03:08.130
(audience applauding)
(Drew laughing)

74
00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:09.920
We welcome that.
Yes, please.

75
00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:11.340 line:15%
All right everybody, you ready?

76
00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:13.020 line:15%
Yeah.
Let's eat!

77
00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:14.120 line:15%
All right, let's go.

78
00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:17.313 line:15%
Mmm! Okay.

79
00:03:18.596 --> 00:03:20.220 line:15%
This is good.
That's good.

80
00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:23.370 line:15%
The smoke really goes through, like, your nasal passages.

81
00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:26.140 line:15%
It goes into your whole olfactory.

82
00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:27.890
I didn't think it'd be that flavorful.

83
00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:30.400
Yeah, that's incredible.
Okay.

84
00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:34.180 line:15%
All right, Adrian, you're a James Beard Award-winning

85
00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:38.510 line:15%
writer, food writer, you've had your fair share of ribs

86
00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:40.830 line:15%
from over 200 places.

87
00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:44.260
I am so honored to ask you, what do you think of these?

88
00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:45.530
Hey, it's so good to be with you.

89
00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:46.610
So yeah, I like this.

90
00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:49.130 line:15%
This is really delicate, you know, tender.

91
00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:50.940 line:15%
I put some of the grilling sauce that I got on there,

92
00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:52.140
so that added some flavor.

93
00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:54.310
And then I love collard greens,

94
00:03:54.310 --> 00:03:55.780
so I'm glad they threw this in the mix.

95
00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:58.830
This is a very soulful touch, so (indistinct).

96
00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:00.310
Have you tried these yet, the collard greens?

97
00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:03.630
Okay, get in. This is so good.

98
00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:05.220
Oh my gosh.

99
00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:07.663
What is going on in this? It's perfect.

100
00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:10.140
Well done.

101
00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:13.500
Oh, thank you so much, Adrian.

102
00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:18.500
Okay, we're moving on to our next contender

103
00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:22.223
at Charles Vergos Rendezvous, check this out.

104
00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:26.530 line:15%
Hi, Drew. I'm Robert, Head Pitmaster at the Rendezvous.

105
00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:28.930
And I'm Anna, one of the managers at the Rendezvous.

106
00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:30.660 line:15%
In the forties, ribs had been a staple

107
00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:32.720 line:15%
of backyard barbecues in Memphis neighborhoods,

108
00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:34.460
but they weren't served on menus.

109
00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:36.680
At that time, ribs were actually considered scrap meat,

110
00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:37.940
and they were extremely cheap.

111
00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:40.090
And for this reason, Charlie thought he'd try 'em out.

112
00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:41.810
That's when he developed his own style

113
00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:43.840
and what is now known as Memphis-style ribs.

114
00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:45.990
To this day, we still cook the ribs the same way

115
00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:46.890
he cooked them back then.

116
00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:50.070
We cook our ribs in this here pit: 16 inches over a fire.

117
00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:52.460
To preserve the tenderness though, we baste them

118
00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:54.560
in a pickle juice, but once the ribs are done,

119
00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:56.580
before we plate 'em to you, we baste them again

120
00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:58.500
with another baste solution to give them to you

121
00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:00.100
before we put the dry rub on them.

122
00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:01.850
We also provide sauce on the side,

123
00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:04.420
just in case you'd like them a little saucy.

124
00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:06.230
The seasoning on the ribs you're about to eat

125
00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:08.960
is what we've been told is the original dry rub,

126
00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:11.390
and what ultimately makes our ribs.

127
00:05:11.390 --> 00:05:13.110
And our side that you're going to taste

128
00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:14.860
is a mustard, vinegar-based slaw.

129
00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:17.210
It is our family's 100-year-old recipe.

130
00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:18.660
People have enjoyed the Rendezvous restaurant

131
00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:19.770
for seven decades.

132
00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:22.563
We seat over 700 people at a time,

133
00:05:22.563 --> 00:05:24.670
4,000 people on a busy night.

134
00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:27.370
We catered Air Force One a few times,

135
00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:29.760
the Rolling Stones have played in our dining room

136
00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:31.820
and even Elvis have eaten Rendezvous.

137
00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:33.460
We hope you guys enjoy this little taste

138
00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:34.723
of our Memphis barbecue.

139
00:05:35.774 --> 00:05:36.940
Wow, that history.

140
00:05:36.940 --> 00:05:39.450
Air Force One, Elvis, and the Rolling Stones.

141
00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:40.550
Are you ready, guys?

142
00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:43.680
So ready. This is so different than the last one.

143
00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:44.513
Ready?

144
00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:48.620 line:15%
Oh, wow.
Okay.

145
00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:49.940 line:15%
Oh, that'll wake you up.

146
00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:50.773 line:15%
Mmm.

147
00:05:51.906 --> 00:05:53.670 line:15%
Okay, and you can certainly taste all the spices

148
00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:55.920
in that dry rub; even the texture's different, right?

149
00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:59.180 line:15%
Yeah, and it really has like a nice crust on it.

150
00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:01.450 line:15%
Emily, it's your family tradition

151
00:06:01.450 --> 00:06:03.720
to have a rib fest every year.

152
00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:05.570
What are your thoughts on these ribs?

153
00:06:05.570 --> 00:06:09.560 line:15%
This spice, it just completely permeates the rib

154
00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:12.590 line:15%
and the meat and it just tastes absolutely amazing.

155
00:06:12.590 --> 00:06:15.240 line:15%
It's not too overwhelming,

156
00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:18.210 line:15%
but it has just such a great flavor to it.

157
00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:19.200
I love it.

158
00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:20.940
Have you guys gone to town on this slaw yet?

159
00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:21.960
I'm obsessed with this.

160
00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:23.960 line:15%
I haven't stopped eating the ribs yet.

161
00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:27.700 line:15%
Let me move over. I feel so.

162
00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:28.650 line:15%
Get ready.

163
00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:32.310
Ooh!
I know, I know.

164
00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:38.760
What's really great is how crunchy the slaw is, yeah.

165
00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:39.813
Really good.

166
00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:45.320 line:15%
Emily, thank you so much for your permeating,

167
00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:48.160 line:15%
especially, word, that's one of my favorites.

168
00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:49.630
My honor, please.

169
00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:52.910
Now we've already tried ribs from Kings Restaurant

170
00:06:52.910 --> 00:06:57.000
in North Carolina, from Charles Vergos from Tennessee,

171
00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:00.120
and now we get to do Joe's Kansas City Bar-B-Que

172
00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:02.113
from Kansas; check this out.

173
00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:05.560 line:15%
Hey Drew, hey Ross, Eric Tadda here,

174
00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:07.830 line:15%
with Joe's Kansas City Bar-B-Que in Kansas City.

175
00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:10.680
Joe's Kansas City Bar-B-Que started in the nineties

176
00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:12.860
when our owner, Jeff and Joy Stehney,

177
00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:14.550
were on the championship barbecue circuit,

178
00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:16.300
and they absolutely fell in love

179
00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:18.720
with serving championship barbecue here in Kansas City.

180
00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:21.100
Joe's KC has been a staple here in Kansas City

181
00:07:21.100 --> 00:07:22.830
for almost 25 years.

182
00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:25.190
And almost just as famous as our barbecue

183
00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:28.230
is where we serve our barbecue, in a gas station.

184
00:07:28.230 --> 00:07:30.860
Joe's has been fortunate to serve our barbecue

185
00:07:30.860 --> 00:07:33.790
to President Obama and the staff on Air Force One.

186
00:07:33.790 --> 00:07:37.170
We have two hometown customers in Jason Sudeikis

187
00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:40.330
and Paul Rudd, and the great chef Anthony Bourdain

188
00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:42.510
once said that Joe's KC Bar-B-Que

189
00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:45.360
is one of the 13 places you must eat before you die.

190
00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:48.327
This year alone, Joe's KC is going to sell and ship

191
00:07:48.327 --> 00:07:52.670
172,000 slabs of ribs nationwide.

192
00:07:52.670 --> 00:07:55.270
We think our ribs are unique because of how we cook them.

193
00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:59.680
We cook them low and slow at 245 degrees for four hours

194
00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:01.570
over Missouri white oak.

195
00:08:01.570 --> 00:08:05.700
So Drew, Ross, we are super excited to be part of this day.

196
00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:08.700
We also included some of our championship barbecue beans.

197
00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:10.170
Got a little spice for your palate.

198
00:08:10.170 --> 00:08:11.466
I think you're going to enjoy.

199
00:08:11.466 --> 00:08:12.920
(audience applauding)

200
00:08:12.920 --> 00:08:15.273
Okay, everybody, you ready? Let's eat.

201
00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:20.950 line:15%
Very different.

202
00:08:20.950 --> 00:08:23.080 line:15%
So different than the other two.

203
00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:26.160 line:15%
But, like that really beautiful,

204
00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:29.580 line:15%
kind of what you really think is like a barbecue sauce.

205
00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:32.610 line:15%
There's certainly layers in this one, yum.

206
00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:34.380 line:15%
You're absolutely right, Ross.

207
00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:37.430
Mable, you were born and raised in the South,

208
00:08:37.430 --> 00:08:40.440 line:15%
and you consider yourself authority on barbecue ribs.

209
00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:42.433 line:15%
Will you please give us your thoughts?

210
00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:47.628
Well, Drew, I have to tell you, this rib is good!

211
00:08:47.628 --> 00:08:51.903 line:15%
It's tangy, it's really easy to come off of the bone,

212
00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:55.380
the meat is really tender.

213
00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:58.440 line:15%
And the sauce, the sauce is so amazing.

214
00:08:58.440 --> 00:08:59.640 line:15%
It's right on point, Drew.

215
00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:02.150 line:15%
It's really, really good.

216
00:09:02.150 --> 00:09:05.430 line:15%
I think you're absolutely right about that sauce, Mable.

217
00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:09.100
They're all very different.

218
00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:10.950
So different.

219
00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:12.690
Okay, well, it's time to vote.

220
00:09:12.690 --> 00:09:13.890
This is going to be really hard.

221
00:09:13.890 --> 00:09:18.340
Before we do, we asked you all to vote on Twitter

222
00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:21.160
to say who your favorite rib joint was

223
00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:23.460
from all these extraordinary places.

224
00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:28.460
And 61% of the vote was Joe's Bar-B-Que, Kansas City!

225
00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:31.050
I can see why.

226
00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:34.140
So that's one vote for Joe's Kansas City Bar-B-Que.

227
00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:36.670
I love that they're in like that Shamrock gas station,

228
00:09:36.670 --> 00:09:39.310
that is so cool, that makes you feel like such a foodie.

229
00:09:39.310 --> 00:09:41.340
Okay, and now it's our turn.

230
00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:42.470
All right, one at a time,

231
00:09:42.470 --> 00:09:44.860
we're going to reveal our favorite.

232
00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:46.693
So kick us off, Adrian.

233
00:09:48.328 --> 00:09:50.020
(victorious music)

234
00:09:50.020 --> 00:09:51.620
Wow, Joe's Kansas City.

235
00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:54.610
I really, really trust your palate.

236
00:09:54.610 --> 00:09:57.760
Okay, beautiful Emily, what is your vote?

237
00:09:57.760 --> 00:09:59.750
Joe's was phenomenal, I agree,

238
00:09:59.750 --> 00:10:02.099
but I have to go with Kings.

239
00:10:02.099 --> 00:10:04.090
(victorious music)
(audience applauding)

240
00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:08.410
Okay, stunning, smart Mable, what is your vote?

241
00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:10.960
Kansas City, of course. Kansas City, here I come.

242
00:10:12.145 --> 00:10:13.407
(Drew squealing)

243
00:10:13.407 --> 00:10:18.090
All right, oh my gosh, that's three for Joe's.

244
00:10:18.090 --> 00:10:19.400
Ross, it's your turn.

245
00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:21.770
Okay, listen, I loved each one of these.

246
00:10:21.770 --> 00:10:23.640
I don't have a bone to pick with any of them,

247
00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:25.960
but one of them has to win

248
00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:27.890
and I have to say it was the tenderness

249
00:10:27.890 --> 00:10:30.640
and the side dish of collard greens

250
00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:33.510
that put Kings Restaurant over the road for me.

251
00:10:33.510 --> 00:10:34.543
It was so good.

252
00:10:37.590 --> 00:10:39.113
I have to do Kings, too.

253
00:10:41.170 --> 00:10:45.350 line:15%
I was really not expecting how much flavor

254
00:10:45.350 --> 00:10:46.560 line:15%
